{"authored" [{"question" "q-21067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,-1),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-4,-8)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-8),$ $(-1,-2)$, and $(0,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "