.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/fee3415af228b80bb5d7c1a68475043fce324234766d243c928fe9eb98e0b900.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-147649" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point, different from the origin"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A quadrilateral"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point, different from the origin"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rectangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/ja